hide(UselessParameterEmptyShellToDumpParameterNotes);
parameters{


		logical	UselessParameterEmptyShellToDumpParameterNotes; //EN The only raison d'etre of this note is that modgen does not like empty parameters statements



	/* DUMP real parameter notes below
   1. remove  >?
   2. replace >"tab   (that is >"	) by >*
   3. replace symbol for le by <=
   4. replace symbol for ne by !=
   5. replace bullet by *

	   */

	   //icitte see parameter "..." rather than see parameter pImmig


  //==============================================

/* NOTE (PaDispatcher, EN )
>
>PARAMETER	Physical activity dispatcher
>LOCATION	Base(Physical_activity).dat
>LAST UPDATE	January 2021
>
>DESCRIPTION	This parameter allows one to specify physical activity
interventions that may depend on sex, age, calendar year and body mass index
(BMI).
>
>DIMENSIONS
>Characteristic of intervention program, Intervention program element
>
>
>SOURCE
>
>DEFINITION
>
>METHODS	This parameter is consulted every two years in POHEM, provided that
the parameter “Switch for physical activity intervention” is turned on.
>The “consultation” of this parameter consists in picking up the first
column/element that applies to the simulated person at a given time.  A column
"applies" if all of the following conditions are met
>*	sex: If the line “Sex (0=female, 1=male, 2=both)” contains 0, it means that
the corresponding column is restricted to females; if it contains 1, it means
that the corresponding column is restricted to males. If the line contains 2,
it means that the corresponding column applies to males and females.
>*	age  range: person is in the age range described by lines "Age start" and
"Age end" (i.e. "Age start"<= age <= "Age end"). Note that age is integer.
>*	time range: current calendar year is in the range described by lines "Year
start" and "Year end" (i.e. "Year start"<= year <= "Year end"). Note that year is
integer.
>*	BMI  range: person is in the BMI range described by lines "BMI lower bound"
and "BMI upper bound" (i.e. "BMI lower bound” <= BMI <= "BMI upper bound").
>
>If none of the columns applies, the person will not be subject to a physical
activity intervention/alteration at this particular moment (time at which
dispatcher is being consulted).  Otherwise, the selected column will lead to
assigning the person to an intervention group (last line of the dispatcher).
The eligible groups are 1, 2, 3, 4, or 5.  This intervention group will be used
to grab the proper slice of the following four input parameters which will then
carry out the desired physical activity alteration.
>1.	transition matrix to implement intervention on leisure time physical
activity
>2.	transition matrix to implement intervention on usual day level of activity
>3.	transition matrix to implement intervention on walking for errands
>4.	transition matrix to implement intervention on biking for errands
>
>ASSUMPTIONS
>
>DECISIONS
>
>REFERENCES
>
>COMMENTS
>
>
>*/

  /* NOTE (MortHazard, EN )
  >PARAMETER	Cohort mortality rates
  >LOCATION	Base(MortalityDisease).dat
  >LAST UPDATE	November 2014
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	This parameter is the baseline mortality hazard of the Canadian
  population.
  >DIMENSIONS
  >[SEX][YEAR][LIFE_SPAN]
  >
  >
  >SOURCE	The parameter was obtained from LifePaths 4.1, however the parameter
  was ultimately derived from cohort life tables published in "New Birth Cohort
  Life Tables for Canada and Quebec, 1801-1991" by Robert Bourbeau, Jacques
  Legare and Valerie Emond, Statistics Canada Catalog 91F0015MPE. The published
  abridged (5 year) life tables were the source of cumulative hazard estimates.
  >
  >Incomplete cohort life tables are projected using mortality assumptions from
  the Statistics Canada Medium official population projections, obtained from the
  demography Division, for the period 2000-2026.  Cohort life tables that are
  still incomplete (i.e., in the period after 2026) have been extrapolated using
  the average rate of mortality improvement - by sex - from the projection period
  2000-2026.  Mortality remains fixed at the levels of the 2026 birth cohort for
  all cohorts born after 2026.
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS	The single year hazards were obtained by differencing single year
  estimates of the cumulative hazards which, in turn, were obtained by clamped,
  cubic-spline interpolation of the abridged cumulative hazards.
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS	The assumptions from the Statistics Canada Medium population
  projections change by projection period.
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >RELATED PARAMETERS	PeriodMortHazardLOW
  >PeriodMortHazardMID
  >PeriodMortHazardHIGH
  >COMMENTS	This parameter can be updated in April 2015 when new mortality
  hazards will be available from DYSEM (SAMD). Only medium term projections will
  be available from 2011 onwards.
  >
  >
  >
  >
  >
  >
  >
  >
  >
  >*//* NOTE (PeriodMortHazardLOW, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Period mortality rates update - Low life expectancy scenario
  >LOCATION
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	This parameter represents the most recent observed mortality
  (1972-2004) appended to the most recent projected period mortality (2005-2051)
  corresponding to LOW life expectancy assumptions.  	These values are used to
  update extrapolated cohort mortality rates (MortHazard) in PreSimulation.
  >
  >DIMENSIONS	[SEX][LIFE_SPAN][PERIOD_UPDATE]
  >
  >SOURCE	The parameter was obtained from LifePaths 4.1
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >
  >
  >*//* NOTE (PeriodMortHazardMID, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Period mortality rates update - Medium life expectancy scenario
  >LOCATION
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	This parameter represents the most recent observed mortality
  (1972-2004) appended to the most recent projected period mortality (2005-2051)
  corresponding to MEDIUM life expectancy assumptions.  These values are used to
  update extrapolated cohort mortality rates (MortHazard) in PreSimulation.
  >
  >DIMENSIONS	[SEX][LIFE_SPAN][PERIOD_UPDATE]
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >
  >
  >*//* NOTE (PeriodMortHazardHIGH, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Period mortality rates update - High life expectancy scenario
  >LOCATION
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	This parameter represents the most recent observed mortality
  (1972-2004) appended to the most recent projected period mortality (2005-2051)
  corresponding to HIGH life expectancy assumptions. These values are used to
  update extrapolated cohort mortality rates (MortHazard) in PreSimulation.
  >
  >DIMENSIONS	[SEX][LIFE_SPAN][PERIOD_UPDATE]
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >
  >
  >*//* NOTE (CauseOfDeath, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Distribution of cause of death
  >LOCATION
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	This parameter is used to assign a cause of death for diseases not
  explicitly modeled.
  >For diseases explicitly modeled which create their own case-fatality, their
  proportion
  >of mortality is subtracted from the lifetable mortality hazards based on the
  contribution shown in this table
  >
  >DIMENSIONS	[SEX][COD_AGE_GROUP][DISEASES]
  >
  >
  >SOURCE	Data Source: Vital Statistics, Death October 1999 (IRMA)
  >These values copied from previous versions of POHEM with this comment.
  >"colon=0, rectal=0 included in other cancer so that disease incidence can be
  modelled without explicit mortality Cause of Death updated with EC, Colon &
  Rectal Cancer from vital statistics October 1999
  > J:\1991 Life Tables\[CauseOfDeath.v3000.xls]COD Distribution 909192m"
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS	Related parameters: pStartYear, pLifetable, MortHazard
  >
  >*//* NOTE (pDiabetes_RR_Mortality, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Diabetes mortality relative risk: non-spline coefficients
  >LOCATION	Base(Diabetes).dat
  >LAST UPDATE	Jan 2015
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	Coefficients for the non-spline portion of the diabetes relative
  risk of mortality
  >
  >DIMENSIONS	[DIABETES_RECENCY][DIABETES_RR_MORTALITY]
  >
  >SOURCE	Data from the Ontario Diabetes Database (ODD) and Ontario Registered
  Persons Database (RPD) available through ICES.
  >
  >DEFINITION	Diabetes recency is the number of years since becoming an incident
  case. After 2 years, a diabetes case becomes remote.
  >
  >METHODS	Relative risk (RR) of all cause death in diabetics in Ontario compared
  to non-diabetics was modeled. The RR is calculated by a function sex, age and
  year and depends on diabetes recency as defined above.
  >
  >The model involved cubic splines with respect to age and year as well as
  non-spline covariates which are found, respectively, in “Diabetes mortality
  relative risk: age cubic spline coefficients”, “Diabetes mortality relative
  risk: year cubic spline coefficients”, and “Diabetes mortality relative risk:
  non-spline coefficients”. Note that the implementation of cubic splines also
  requires parameters “Diabetes mortality relative risk: age cubic spline
  companion” and “Diabetes mortality relative risk: year cubic spline companion”.
  >
  >If a person is diabetic, his/her hazard is elevated/altered as follows
  >hazardDIABETIC / hazardNonDIABETIC  = b0 + b1 sex + b2 min(age,96)
  >                                          +LT(age)+LT(year)
  >                                          +CS(age)+CS(year)
  >The parameter “Diabetes mortality relative risk: non-spline coefficients”
  contains (b0, b1, b2) and are “diabetes recency” specific. In the above, age is
  integer age, year is calendar year.  Below, any of those two is denoted by
  time.  LT and CS described below are “diabetes recency” specific but this is
  not reflected in notation to ease reading.
  >
  >LT(time) = coefficient * (coerced(time) – (A+B)C/2) where coerced(time) is
  time restricted to be no less than A and no more than B, i.e.
  coerce(time)=max(min(time,B),A). In the above, C is 1 or 0 so that
  coerced(time) is centered (C=1) or not (C=0).  Note that centering applies to
  LT(time) only, not to CS(time) discussed below.  The parameter “Diabetes
  mortality relative risk: <time> cubic spline companion” contains A, B, C and
  the coefficient.
  >CS(time) = sum coefficient(k) * CS(coerced(time)/scale,k) with CS(time,k)
  being the kth restricted cubic spline and coefficient(k) being the
  corresponding coefficient. The scale is the range of knots (largest minus
  smallest) raised to the power found in the aforementioned “companion”
  parameter.  The sum is taken over the first K-2 knots where K is the total
  number of knots provided. The parameter “Diabetes mortality relative risk:
  <time> cubic spline coefficients” provides the location of the K knots
  (location is assumed to be non-negative) and the coefficients whereas
  coerced(time) is the same as what was explained in the description of LT(time)
  above.
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES	Restricted Cubic Spline Regression: A Brief Introduction
  https://support.sas.com/resources/papers/proceedings16/5621-2016.pdf
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >
  >
  >*/

  /* NOTE (DiabetesMortalityRelRiskAgeSplineLocationCoef, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Diabetes mortality relative risk: age cubic spline coefficients
  >LOCATION	Base(Diabetes).dat
  >LAST UPDATE	Jan 2015
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	Coefficients for the age cubic spline portion of the diabetes
  relative risk of mortality
  >
  >DIMENSIONS	Diabetes recency, Cubic spline location/coefficient, Knot index
  >
  >SOURCE	Data from the Ontario Diabetes Database (ODD) and Ontario Registered
  Persons Database (RPD) available through ICES.
  >
  >DEFINITION	Diabetes recency is the number of years since becoming an incident
  case. After 2 years a diabetes case becomes remote.
  >
  >METHODS	Relative risk (RR) of all cause death in diabetics in Ontario compared
  to non-diabetics was modeled. The RR is calculated by a function sex, age and
  year and depends on diabetes recency as defined above.
  >The model involved cubic splines with respect to age and year as well as
  non-spline covariates which are found, respectively, in "Diabetes mortality
  relative risk: age cubic spline coefficients", "Diabetes mortality relative
  risk: year cubic spline coefficients", "Diabetes mortality relative risk:
  non-spline coefficients". Note that the implementation of cubic splines also
  requires parameters "Diabetes mortality relative risk: age cubic spline
  companion" and "Diabetes mortality relative risk: year cubic spline companion".
  >
  >If a person is diabetic, his/her hazard is elevated/altered as follows
  >hazardDIABETIC / hazardNonDIABETIC  = b0 + b1 sex + b2 min(age,96)
  >                                          +LT(age)+LT(year)
  >                                          +CS(age)+CS(year)
  >The parameter "Diabetes mortality relative risk: non-spline coefficients"
  contains (b0, b1, b2) and are "diabetes recency" specific. In the above, age is
  integer age, year is calendar year.  Below, any of those two is denoted by
  time.  LT and CS described below are "diabetes recency" specific but this is
  not reflected in notation to ease reading.
  >
  >LT(time) = coefficient * (coerced(time) - (A+B)C/2) where coerced(time) is
  time restricted to be no less than A and no more than B, i.e.
  coerce(time)=max(min(time,B),A). In the above, C is 1 or 0 so that
  coerced(time) is centered (C=1) or not (C=0).  Note that centering applies to
  LT(time) only, not to CS(time) discussed below.   The parameter "Diabetes
  mortality relative risk: <time>  cubic spline companion" contains A, B, C and
  the coefficient.
  >CS(time) = sum coefficient(k) * CS(coerced(time)/scale,k) with CS(time,k)
  being the kth restricted cubic spline and coefficient(k) being the
  corresponding coefficient. The scale is the range of knots (largest minus
  smallest) raised to the power found in the aforementioned "companion"
  parameter.  The sum is taken over the first K-2 knots where K is the total
  number of knots provided. The parameter "Diabetes mortality relative risk:
  <time>  cubic spline coefficients" provides the location of the K knots
  (location is assumed to be non-negative) and the coefficients whereas
  coerced(time) is the same as what was explained in the description of LT(time)
  above.
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES	Restricted Cubic Spline Regression: A Brief Introduction
  https://support.sas.com/resources/papers/proceedings16/5621-2016.pdf
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >
  >
  >*//* NOTE (DiabetesMortalityRelRiskAgeSplineCompanion, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Diabetes mortality relative risk: age cubic spline companion
  >LOCATION	Base(Diabetes).dat
  >LAST UPDATE	Jan 2015
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	Companion for the age cubic spline portion of the diabetes
  relative risk of mortality
  >
  >DIMENSIONS	Diabetes recency, Cubic spline companion
  >
  >SOURCE	Data from the Ontario Diabetes Database (ODD) and Ontario Registered
  Persons Database (RPD) available through ICES.
  >
  >DEFINITION	Diabetes recency is the number of years since becoming an incident
  case. After 2 years a diabetes case becomes remote.
  >
  >METHODS	Relative risk (RR) of all cause death in diabetics in Ontario compared
  to non-diabetics was modeled. The RR is calculated by a function sex, age and
  year and depends on diabetes recency as defined above.
  >The model involved cubic splines with respect to age and year as well as
  non-spline covariates which are found, respectively, in "Diabetes mortality
  relative risk: age cubic spline coefficients", "Diabetes mortality relative
  risk: year cubic spline coefficients", "Diabetes mortality relative risk:
  non-spline coefficients". Note that the implementation of cubic splines also
  requires parameters "Diabetes mortality relative risk: age cubic spline
  companion" and "Diabetes mortality relative risk: year cubic spline companion".
  >
  >If a person is diabetic, his/her hazard is elevated/altered as follows
  >hazardDIABETIC / hazardNonDIABETIC  = b0 + b1 sex + b2 min(age,96)
  >                                          +LT(age)+LT(year)
  >                                          +CS(age)+CS(year)
  >The parameter "Diabetes mortality relative risk: non-spline coefficients"
  contains (b0, b1, b2) and are "diabetes recency" specific. In the above, age is
  integer age, year is calendar year.  Below, any of those two is denoted by
  time.  LT and CS described below are "diabetes recency" specific but this is
  not reflected in notation to ease reading.
  >
  >LT(time) = coefficient * (coerced(time) - (A+B)C/2) where coerced(time) is
  time restricted to be no less than A and no more than B, i.e.
  coerce(time)=max(min(time,B),A). In the above, C is 1 or 0 so that
  coerced(time) is centered (C=1) or not (C=0).  Note that centering applies to
  LT(time) only, not to CS(time) discussed below.   The parameter "Diabetes
  mortality relative risk: <time>  cubic spline companion" contains A, B, C and
  the coefficient.
  >CS(time) = sum coefficient(k) * CS(coerced(time)/scale,k) with CS(time,k)
  being the kth restricted cubic spline and coefficient(k) being the
  corresponding coefficient. The scale is the range of knots (largest minus
  smallest) raised to the power found in the aforementioned "companion"
  parameter.  The sum is taken over the first K-2 knots where K is the total
  number of knots provided. The parameter "Diabetes mortality relative risk:
  <time>  cubic spline coefficients" provides the location of the K knots
  (location is assumed to be non-negative) and the coefficients whereas
  coerced(time) is the same as what was explained in the description of LT(time)
  above.
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES	Restricted Cubic Spline Regression: A Brief Introduction
  https://support.sas.com/resources/papers/proceedings16/5621-2016.pdf
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >
  >
  >*//* NOTE (DiabetesMortalityRelRiskYearSplineLocationCoef, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Diabetes mortality relative risk: year cubic spline coefficients
  >LOCATION	Base(Diabetes).dat
  >LAST UPDATE	Jan 2015
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	Coefficients for the year cubic spline portion of the diabetes
  relative risk of mortality
  >
  >DIMENSIONS	Diabetes recency, Cubic spline location/coefficient, Knot index
  >
  >SOURCE	Data from the Ontario Diabetes Database (ODD) and Ontario Registered
  Persons Database (RPD) available through ICES.
  >
  >DEFINITION	Diabetes recency is the number of years since becoming an incident
  case. After 2 years a diabetes case becomes remote.
  >
  >METHODS	Relative risk (RR) of all cause death in diabetics in Ontario compared
