  /* NOTE (MaxYear, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Year simulation ends
  >LOCATION	Base(MortalityDisease).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	This is the year beyond which nobody can live. The hecatomb occurs
  at the beginning of the year, so if the parameter value is 2042 then everybody
  who lived through 2041 will die on 2042's new year eve.
  >
  >DIMENSIONS	None
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >

  >*//* NOTE (MaxLife, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Maximum number of years of life
  >LOCATION	Base(MortalityDisease).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	This is the age beyond which nobody can live. So if the parameter
  is 100 then the maximum age one can reach is 100 years, 0 day, 0 second.
  >
  >DIMENSIONS	None
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >

  >*/


  /* NOTE (pPOPULATION, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Population by sex, age, province (territory) and year
  >LOCATION	Base(Births).dat
  >LAST UPDATE	September 2020
  >
  >DESCRIPTION
  >
  >DIMENSIONS	[YEARS][SEX][cPROVINCE][LIFE_SPAN]
  >
  >
  >SOURCE	All values (by year, sex and province/territory, age) populated as
  follows:
  >* Observed estimates for 2001-2017 from CANSIM Table 17-10-0005-01 (formerly
  Table 051-0001)
  >* Projected (M3) estimates for 2018-2043 (see ASSUMPTIONS)
  >* Imputed estimates for 2044-2100 based on 2043 estimates
  >* Zero values for age groups 100-119 for all years, sexes and
  provinces/territories
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS	The population parameter has three functions/purposes:
  >(1) The first is to help assign an exact age from the age group of the case
  being initialized from the StartPop input parameter. (see parameter
  pUseInputPopulationToAssignAge)
  >(2) It is used in the projection of new cases ("births" and immigrants) in
  future years.
  >(3) It can be used to "scale" the results to the population distribution (by
  age, sex and province, although this isn't necessary if the input database
  StartPop is representative of the population.
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >The M3 projections (The medium-growth and 1999/2000 to 2002/2003
  interprovincial migrations trends) scenario is defined by the following
  assumptions:
  >(i) Canadian total fertility rate that reaches 1.67 births per woman in
  2021/2022 and remains constant thereafter;
  >(ii) Canadian life expectancy that reaches 87.5 years for males and 89.1 years
  for females in 2062/2063;
  >(iii) interprovincial migration based on the trends observed between 1999/2000
  and 2002/2003;
  >(iv) national immigration rate that reaches 0.75% in 2022/2023 and remains
  constant thereafter;
  >(v) annual number of non-permanent residents (Canada) that reaches 864,600 in
  2021 and remains constant thereafter;
  >(vi) National net emigration rate of 0.19%.
  >The above is from demography documentation (may be outdated, was probably
  correct circa November 2014)
  >
  >DECISIONS	In November 2014, we changed the dimensions of this parameter
  beginning in 1971 to begin in 2001.
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >
  >*/
  
  /* NOTE (FictivePersonCharacteristics, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Characteristics of a fictive person to be simulated
  >LOCATION	Base(StartPop_Control).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	This parameter may be used to run POHEM in clinical mode (rather
  than population mode as POHEM's PO suggests – recall POHEM stands for
  POpulation HEalth Model).  It may also be useful for debugging/probing
  purposes.
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >[DB_FIELDS]
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION	The codesets (e.g. Sex=1 corresponds to male) are found below
  >Five year age group : 2 = <20, 3 = 20-24, 4 = 25-29, 5 = 30-34, 6 = 35-39, ...
  ,  13 = 70-74, 14 = 75-79, 15 = 80+
  >Sex : 0 = Female, 1 = Male
  >Province : 0 = Newfoundland, 1 = PEI, 2 = Nova Scotia, 3 = New Brunswick, 4 =
  Quebec, 5 = Ontario, 6 = Manitoba, 7 = Saskatchewan, 8 = Alberta, 9 = British
  Columbia
  >Ethnicity : 0 = White, 1 = Non-white
  >Cultural Origin : 0 = White, 1 = Aboriginal, 2 = Black, 3 = Chinese, 4 = South
  asian, 5 = Japanese or Korean, 6 = South east asian, 7 = Unknown (see comments
  at bottom of these parameter notes)
  >Immigrant : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Recent immigrant (last 9 years) : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Education : 0 = Less than secondary, 1 = Secondary grad , 2 = Other
  post-secondary, 3 = Post-secondary grad
  >Income : 0 = Quartile 1 (poorest), 1 = Quartile 2 (lower middle), 2 = Quartile
  3 (upper middle), 3 = quartile 4 (richest)
  >Alcohol drinks per week (dpw) : 0 = Never drank, 1 = Former drinker, 2 = light
  (< 2 dpw), 3 = moderate (2-14 dpw for male, 2-9 dpw for female), 4 = heavy (>14
  dpw for male, > 9 dpw for female)
  >Body mass index in kg/(m^2)
  >Height in meters
  >Regular smoker : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Current/Former smoker : 0 = Never smoker, 1 = Former smoker, 2 = Current
  smoker
  >Arthritis : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >OsteoArthritis : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Had heart attack : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Angina : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Congestive heart failure : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Stroke : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >HUI PUMF component vision problem : 1 = No, 2 = corrected by lenses, 3 =
  distant not corrected, 4 = close not corrected, 5 = Problem seeing close &
  distant / no sight
  >HUI PUMF component hearing problem :  1 = No, 2 = corrected, 3 = not corrected
  >HUI PUMF component speech problem : 1 = No, 2 = partially/not understood
  >HUI PUMF component mobility problem : 1 = No, 2 = Yes but no aid required, 3 =
  requires support/wheelchair, 4 = requires help/cannot walk
  >HUI PUMF component dexterity problem : 1 = No, 2 = Yes but no help required, 3
  = needs help
  >HUI PUMF component emotional problem : 1 = Happy and interested in life, 2 =
  Somewhat happy, 3 = Somewhat unhappy, 4 = Very unhappy, 5 = So unhappy that
  life is not worthwhile
  >HUI PUMF component cognitive problem : 1 = No, 2 = little difficulty thinking,
  3 = Somewhat forgetful, 4 = Somewhat forgetful/little difficulty thinking, 5 =
  Very forgetful/great difficulty thinking, 6 = Unable to remember/think
  >HUI PUMF component pain problem : 1 = No, 2 = Mild/moderate pain does not
  prevent activity, 3 = Moderate pain prevents a few activities, 4 =
  Moderate/severe pain prevents some activities, 5 = Severe pain prevents most
  activities
  >HUI
  >SPoRT component physical activity : 0 = active, 1 = moderate, 2 = inactive, 3
  = not stated
  >SPoRT component smoking : 0 = 20+ daily cigarettes, 1 = <20 daily cigarettes
  or former daily smoker now occasional or never daily smoker , 2 = Former daily
  , 3 = Former occasional , 4 = Missing
  >SPoRT component diet (vegetable & carrot) : 0 = 0-6 weekly, 1 = 7-13 weekly, 2
  = 14+ weekly but not extreme, 3 = extreme ( > 140 carrots weekly or > 140
  vegetable weekly) or missing
  >SPoRT component life stressful : 0 = not at all, 1 = not very, 2 = a bit, 3 =
  quite a bit, 4 = extremely, 5 = missing
  >SPoRT component heart disease : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >SPoRT component diabetes : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >SPoRT component hypertension : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >SPoRT component alcohol : 0 = heavy, 1 = medium, 2 = light, 3 = occasional, 4
  = former never, 5 = binge, 6 = missing
  >Smoke PackYears
  >Has cancer : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Number of chronic conditions among following 16: a) food allergies, b) other
  allergies, c) asthma, d) fibromyalgia, e) back problems, f) migraine, g)
  bronchitis/emphysema/COPD , h) epilepsy, i) stomach/intestinal ulcers, j)
  stroke, k) incontinence, l) bowel disorder/Crohn Disease or colitis, m)
  Alzheimer/dementia (censored PUMF), n) cataracts, o) glaucoma, p) thyroid
  condition
  >Walk for errands : 0 = No, 1 = 0 to 5 hrs/week, 2 = 6 to 10 hrs/week, 3 = 11+
  hrs/week
  >Bike for errands : 0 = None, 1 = Some
  >Level of physical activity for usual day : 0 = Sit, 1 = Stand or walk quite a
  lot , 2 = Lift/carry light , 3 =  heavy work/carry very heavy loads
  >Leisure time physical activity : hours/day (e.g. 0.45 hour/day)
  >Survey weight
  >
  >METHODS	One needs to fill the CCHS-like questionnaire above.  The clinical
  mode gets activated if a positive value is provided in the last question
  (survey weight); a negative value calls for regular POHEM (population mode).
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS	Regarding Cultural Origin, the codeset suggests 8 different
  categories.  In POHEM, this information is only used to differentiate between
  Aboriginal and others.  Therefore, if one uses this parameter to create a
  fictive person, it will behave the same if Cultural Origin is set to 0 = White
  or 2 = Black or 3 = Chinese or 4 = South asian or 5 = Japanese or Korean or 6 =
  South east asian or 7 = Unknown.  Only the setting 1 = Aboriginal may lead to a
  different lifepath.
  >
  >*/
  /* NOTE (pImmig, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Number of new immigrants by sex, age, province and year
  >LOCATION	Base(Immig).dat
  >LAST UPDATE	November 2020
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	Number of new immigrants by sex, age, province and year (aka
  pImmig) provides POHEM with the number of immigrants by year, sex, province and
  age. This parameter is used to generate the correct number of immigrants after
  2001. In 2001 the number of immigrants is determined by the starting
  population.
  >More details in the SOURCE section below.
  >
  >DIMENSIONS	[YEARS][SEX][cPROVINCE][LIFE]
  >
  >SOURCE	All values (by year, sex and province/territory, age) populated as
  follows:
  >* Observed estimates for 2001-2018 from CANSIM Table 17-10-0014-01 (formerly
  Table 051-0011)
  >* Projected (M3) estimates for 2019-2042 (see ASSUMPTIONS)
  >* Imputed estimates for 2043-2100 based on 2042 estimates
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS	This parameter is used to determine the distribution of new immigrants
  in future years (after pStartYear up to pMaxYearNewArrivals) by sex, age and
  year of immigration.
  >
  >The remaining characteristics of the immigrants are assigned through hot-deck
  imputation of immigrants (within the past 9 years) represented in the
  initialization database.
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS	For years 2019-2042, used official population projections from the
  cohort component model for ages 0-110. These were the medium term projections
  M3. According to the demography documentation, the medium-growth and 1999/2000
  to 2002/2003 interprovincial migrations trends scenario is defined by the
  following assumptions:
  >(i) a Canadian total fertility rate that reaches 1.67 births per woman in
  2021/2022 and remains constant thereafter;
  >(ii) a Canadian life expectancy that reaches 87.5 years for males and 89.1
  years for females in 2062/2063;
  >(iii) interprovincial migration based on the trends observed between 1999/2000
  and 2002/2003;
  >(iv) a national immigration rate that reaches 0.75% in 2022/2023 and remains
  constant thereafter;
  >(v) an annual number of non-permanent residents (Canada) that reaches 864,600
  in 2021 and remains constant thereafter;
  >(vi) National net emigration rate of 0.19%.
  >
  >>Data Source: Demography Division, Statistics Canada. Obtained from Simone
  Coulomb in November 2014
  >The above is from Demography Division (Simone Coulomb in November 2014). It
  may now be outdated.
  >
  >DECISIONS	In November 2014, we changed the dimensions of this parameter
  beginning in 1971 to begin in 2001.
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >
  >
  >
  >*//* NOTE (pStartPop, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Distribution of population at beginning of simulation
  >LOCATION
  >LAST UPDATE	January 2019.  Reverted back to the 20160621 version (prior to
  addition of veterans)
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	This contains micro data from the CCHS PUMF 2001 for about 50
  variables on respondents aged 20+.  Some records are removed due to missing
  values.
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >[DB_RECORDS][DB_FIELDS]
  >
  >SOURCE	Mostly CCHS PUMF 2001.
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS	Dummy records are filled with -1 in all fields.
  >See parameter “Characteristics of a fictive person to be simulated” for the
  list of fields/variables. Regarding Cultural Origin, it is always white in this
  parameter because it is based on the PUMF which does not provide this
  information (unlike the CCHS master file which was used in the distant past).
  >
  >
  >*/
  /* NOTE (pNewArrivalsControl, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Open or Closed population
  >LOCATION
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	This parameter determines which type of cases will be generated by
  the simulation.
  >
  >If "StartPop database" is checked then cases will be generated in the start
  year (pStartYear) of the simulation according to the cross-sectional
  representation of the population found in the initialization database (see
  parameter pStartPop).
  >
  >If "New Births" is checked then new cases will be generated in future years
  (up to pMaxYearNewArrivals) to reflect the growth in the Canadian population.
  Note that these cases are generated at a specific age equal to the youngest age
  in pStartPop (uses parameter pPOPULATION)
  >
  >If "New immigrants" is checked then new cases will be generated in future
  years (up to pMaxYearNewArrivals) to reflect projected immigration patterns
  (see parameter pImmig)
  >
  >
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >[cPopulationSource]
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS	There are 8 possible ways to populate this parameter.
  >

  >*/

  /* NOTE (pMaxYearNewArrivals, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Last year for generating Future Births and Immigrants
  >LOCATION	Base(StartPop_Control).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	The last year for generating Future Births and Immigrants. It is used
  to decide how many cases are needed.
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >None
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS	Relations to other parameters
  >Should be greater than or equal to pStartYear.
  >There is also a parameter called "Year simulation ends" (aka MaxYear) which
  allows lives to be lived sufficiently long for lifetime outcomes like life
  expectancy.
  >

  >*//* NOTE (ForceInitialisationToNonImmigrant, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Set actor initial immigration status to non-immigrant
  >LOCATION	Base(StartPop_Control).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	Select TRUE (checked box) for each of POHEM's point of entry where
  you would want the initial immigrant status to be set to non-immigrant
  regardless of the actual donor immigrant status. This does not impact how
  donors are selected; it just overwrites the immigrant status after the
  selection of the donor.
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >[cPopulationSource]
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION	In POHEM, there are 3 points of entry: (i) 2001, (ii) new births
  after 2001, and (iii) new immigrants after 2001.
  >
  >METHODS	A simulated case entering through any point of entry will be
  initialised by taking the characteristics of a suitable donor selected at
  random from the donor file ("Distribution of population at beginning of
  simulation"). Such donor may be an immigrant according to immigrant status
  variable. If one had an issue with bringing in immigrants from a given point of
  entry, the parameter "Set actor initial immigration status to non-immigrant"
  currently discussed may be used to reset the immigrant status of the selected
  donor to non-immigrant and then simulate his/her life.
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS	In the default scenario, all boxes are set to FALSE (unchecked) so
  that no overwriting of the donor's immigration status is performed.
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >
  >

  >*//* NOTE (pStartPopReweightDefinitionFields, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Reweight population data base: field
  >LOCATION	Base(StartPop_Control).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >[STARTPOP_SUBPOPULATIONS][CONDITIONS_REWEIGHT]
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS	The parameter "Distribution of population at beginning of simulation"
  (aka pStartPop) comes with survey weights that may be altered through the
  following trio of parameters:
  >A: "Reweight population data base: field" (aka
  pStartPopReweightDefinitionFields)
  >B: "Reweight population data base: acceptance range" (aka
  pStartPopReweightDefinitionBounds)
  >C: "Reweight population data base: multiplier" (aka
  pStartPopReweightMultiplier)
  >
  >Parameters A and B allow one to define up to 9 subpopulations and parameter C
  allows one to specify how survey weights should be altered (multiplicatively)
  for each population. For instance, if parameters A, B and C are set up to (i)
  multiply survey weights of women (subpopulation 1) by 0.9 and (ii) multiply
  survey weights of Albertans (subpopulation 2) by 1.2, then the survey weights
  of female Albertans are multiplied by 0.9*1.2 = 1.08.
  >
  >POHEM conducts its entire business with the altered weights.  So if Lucy the
  female Albertan has survey weight equal to 200 on the donor database (parameter
  "Distribution of population at beginning of simulation"), her survey weight
  would become 216 (based on example above) and POHEM would totally forget that
  Lucy's survey weight ever was 200 at some point.
  >
  >The parameter currently discussed/documented is A: "Reweight population data
  base: field" (aka pStartPopReweightDefinitionFields).
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >

  >*//* NOTE (pStartPopReweightDefinitionBounds, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Reweight population data base: acceptance range
  >LOCATION	Base(StartPop_Control).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >[STARTPOP_SUBPOPULATIONS][CONDITIONS_REWEIGHT][cLOWER_UPPER_BOUNDS]
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION	The codesets (e.g. Sex=1 corresponds to male) are found below
  >Five year age group : 2 = <20, 3 = 20-24, 4 = 25-29, 5 = 30-34, 6 = 35-39, ...
  ,  13 = 70-74, 14 = 75-79, 15 = 80+
  >Sex : 0 = Female, 1 = Male
  >Province : 0 = Newfoundland, 1 = PEI, 2 = Nova Scotia, 3 = New Brunswick, 4 =
  Quebec, 5 = Ontario, 6 = Manitoba, 7 = Saskatchewan, 8 = Alberta, 9 = BC
  >Ethnicity : 0 = White, 1 = Non-white
  >Cultural Origin : 0 = White, 1 = Aboriginal, 2 = Black, 3 = Chinese, 4 = South
  asian, 5 = Japanese or Korean, 6 = South east asian, 7 = Unknown
  >Immigrant : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Recent immigrant (last 9 years) : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Education : 0 = Less than secondary, 1 = Secondary grad , 2 = Other
  post-secondary, 3 = Post-secondary grad
  >Income : 0 = Quartile 1 (poorest), 1 = Quartile 2 (lower middle), 2 = Quartile
  3 (upper middle), 3 = quartile 4 (richest)
  >Alcohol drinks per week (dpw) : 0 = Never drank, 1 = Former drinker, 2 = light
  (< 2 dpw), 3 = moderate (2-14 dpw for male, 2-9 dpw for female), 4 = heavy (>14
  dpw for male, > 9 dpw for female)
  >Body mass index in kg/(m^2)
  >Height in meters
  >Regular smoker : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Current/Former smoker : 0 = Never smoker, 1 = Former smoker, 2 = Current
  smoker
  >Arthritis : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >OsteoArthritis : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Had heart attack : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Angina : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Congestive heart failure : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Stroke : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >HUI PUMF component vision problem : 1 = No, 2 = corrected by lenses, 3 =
  distant not corrected, 4 = close not corrected, 5 = 	Problem seeing close &
  distant / no sight
  >HUI PUMF component hearing problem :  1 = No, 2 = corrected, 3 = not corrected
  >HUI PUMF component speech problem : 1 = No, 2 = partially/not understood
  >HUI PUMF component mobility problem : 1 = No, 2 = Yes but no aid required, 3 =
  requires support/wheelchair, 4 = requires help/cannot walk
  >HUI PUMF component dexterity problem : 1 = No, 2 = Yes but no help required, 3
  = needs help
  >HUI PUMF component emotional problem : 1 = Happy and interested in life, 2 =
  Somewhat happy, 3 = Somewhat unhappy, 4 = Very unhappy, 5 = So unhappy that
  life is not worthwhile
  >HUI PUMF component cognitive problem : 1 = No, 2 = little difficulty thinking,
  3 = Somewhat forgetful, 4 = Somewhat forgetful/little difficulty thinking, 5 =
  Very forgetful/great difficulty thinking, 6 = Unable to remember/think
  >HUI PUMF component pain problem : 1 = No, 2 = Mild/moderate pain does not
  prevent activity, 3 = Moderate pain prevents a few activities, 4 =
  Moderate/severe pain prevents some activities, 5 = Severe pain prevents most
  activities
  >HUI
  >SPoRT component physical activity : 0 = active, 1 = moderate, 2 = inactive, 3
  = not stated
  >SPoRT component smoking : 0 = 20+ daily cigarettes, 1 = <20 daily cigarettes
  or former daily smoker now occasional or never daily smoker , 2 = Former daily
  , 3 = Former occasional , 4 = Missing
  >SPoRT component diet (vegetable & carrot) : 0 = 0-6 weekly, 1 = 7-13 weekly, 2
  = 14+ weekly but not extreme, 3 = extreme ( > 140 carrots weekly or > 140
  vegetable weekly) or missing
  >SPoRT component life stressful : 0 = not at all, 1 = not very, 2 = a bit, 3 =
  quite a bit, 4 = extremely, 5 = missing
  >SPoRT component heart disease : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >SPoRT component diabetes : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >SPoRT component hypertension : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >SPoRT component alcohol : 0 = heavy, 1 = medium, 2 = light, 3 = occasional, 4
  = former never, 5 = binge, 6 = missing
  >Smoke PackYears
  >Has cancer : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Number of chronic conditions among following 16: a) food allergies, b) other
  allergies, c) asthma, d) fibromyalgia, e) back problems, f) migraine, g)
  bronchitis/emphysema/COPD , h) epilepsy, i) stomach/intestinal ulcers, j)
  stroke, k) incontinence, l) bowel disorder/Crohn Disease or colitis, m)
  Alzheimer/dementia (censored PUMF), n) cataracts, o) glaucoma, p) thyroid
  condition
  >Walk for errands : 0 = No, 1 = 0 to 5 hrs/week, 2 = 6 to 10 hrs/week, 3 = 11+
  hrs/week
  >Bike for errands : 0 = None, 1 = Some
  >Level of physical activity for usual day : 0 = Sit, 1 = Stand or walk quite a
  lot , 2 = Lift/carry light , 3 =  heavy work/carry very heavy loads
  >Leisure time physical activity : hours/day (e.g. 0.45 hour/day)
  >Survey weight
  >
  >METHODS	The parameter "Distribution of population at beginning of simulation"
  (aka pStartPop) comes with survey weights that may be altered through the
  following trio of parameters:
  >A: "Reweight population data base: field" (aka
  pStartPopReweightDefinitionFields)
  >B: "Reweight population data base: acceptance range" (aka
  pStartPopReweightDefinitionBounds)
  >C: "Reweight population data base: multiplier" (aka
  pStartPopReweightMultiplier)
  >
  >Parameters A and B allow one to define up to 9 subpopulations and parameter C
  allows one to specify how survey weights should be altered (multiplicatively)
  for each population. For instance, if parameters A, B and C are set up to (i)
  multiply survey weights of women (subpopulation 1) by 0.9 and (ii) multiply
  survey weights of Albertans (subpopulation 2) by 1.2, then the survey weights
  of female Albertans are multiplied by 0.9*1.2 = 1.08.
  >
  >
  >POHEM conducts its entire business with the altered weights.  So if Lucy the
  female Albertan has survey weight equal to 200 on the donor database (parameter
  "Distribution of population at beginning of simulation"), her survey weight
  would become 216 (based on example above) and POHEM would totally forget that
  Lucy's survey weight ever was 200 at some point.
  >
  >The parameter currently discussed/documented is B: "Reweight population data
  base: acceptance range" (aka pStartPopReweightDefinitionBounds).
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS

  >*//* NOTE (pStartPopReweightMultiplier, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Reweight population data base: multiplier
  >LOCATION	Base(StartPop_Control).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >[STARTPOP_SUBPOPULATIONS][DUMMY_ROW_OR_COLUMN]
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS	The parameter "Distribution of population at beginning of simulation"
  (aka pStartPop) comes with survey weights that may be altered through the
  following trio of parameters:
  >A: "Reweight population data base: field" (aka
  pStartPopReweightDefinitionFields)
  >B: "Reweight population data base: acceptance range" (aka
  pStartPopReweightDefinitionBounds)
  >C: "Reweight population data base: multiplier" (aka
  pStartPopReweightMultiplier)
  >
  >Parameters A and B allow one to define up to 9 subpopulations and parameter C
  allows one to specify how survey weights should be altered (multiplicatively)
  for each population. For instance, if parameters A, B and C are set up to (i)
  multiply survey weights of women (subpopulation 1) by 0.9 and (ii) multiply
  survey weights of Albertans (subpopulation 2) by 1.2, then the survey weights
  of female Albertans are multiplied by 0.9*1.2 = 1.08.
  >
  >POHEM conducts its entire business with the altered weights.  So if Lucy the
  female Albertan has survey weight equal to 200 on the donor database (parameter
  "Distribution of population at beginning of simulation"), her survey weight
  would become 216 (based on example above) and POHEM would totally forget that
  Lucy's survey weight ever was 200 at some point.
  >
  >The parameter currently discussed/documented is C: "Reweight population data
  base: multiplier" (aka pStartPopReweightMultiplier).
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
