  >
  >*/

  /* NOTE (UseDport, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Use DPoRT algorithm for diabetes incidence
  >LOCATION	Base(Diseases).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	Use this parameter to indicate if diabetes incidence should be
  driven by DPoRT equation or an alternative equation derived from the
  longitudinal National Population Health Survey (NPHS).
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >None
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS	When POHEM is executed, two diabetes models are run: DPoRT and
  longitudinal NPHS-based alternative.  Those two models are independent (one
  does not impact the other) but involve similar risk factors.  Therefore those
  who are deemed at high risk of diabetes according to DPoRT will likely also be
  deemed to be at elevated risk of diabetes according to the longitudinal
  NPHS-based model.
  >
  >The parameter "Use DPoRT algorithm for diabetes incidence" allows one to say
  which diabetes model will be used in tabulation and other models (e.g. HUI);
  the other model will run possibly without purpose.
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS	The longitudinal NPHS-based diabetes model is part of a suite of
  chronic conditions models that also includes hypertension and heart disease.
  As far as these longitudinal NPHS-based models are concerned (e.g.
  hypertension), the longitudinal NPHS-based diabetes model is used regardless of
  the selection made in the parameter "Use DPoRT algorithm for diabetes
  incidence".
  >
  >*//* NOTE (TimeHorizonForDport, EN )
  >
  >PARAMETER	Time horizon used in the implementation of DPoRT
  >LOCATION	Base(Diabetes).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	This parameter provides the time horizon used to convert DPoRT's
  hazard into annual hazard
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >None
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS	DPoRT provides a hazard of people risk of Diabetes as a function of
  many thing including time since NPHS 1996 interview.  The hazard/risk of
  developing diabetes 1 nanosecond after the interview differs from that of
  developing diabetes 2 nanosecond after the interview.  Though modgen (the
  software/language used to develop POHEM) is totally capable to implement this
  kind of time varying hazard, it was decided to implement DPoRT with annual
  hazard that with be constant between birthdays (ceteris paribus).
  >
  >This is done by calculating the probability that one would develop diabetes
  over K years based on DPoRT's hazard and then figuring out what constant hazard
  over those K years would have led to the same probability.  The outcome of this
  exercise (constant hazard) depends on K which is the value provided by the
  parameter "Time horizon used in the implementation of DPoRT".
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS	K = 2
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >

  >*/

  /* NOTE (DementiaPrevalenceTarget2001, EN )
	JB extrapolated back to 2001- used years 2002 through 2010 and regression methods
	*/


	/* NOTE (DportAlterationSpecs, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER	Alteration of DPoRT linear predictor by sex and age
	>LOCATION	Base(Diabetes).dat
	>LAST UPDATE
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	This parameter introduces an additional age*sex term in DPoRT's
	equation so that POHEM rates of diabetes better align with target  from
	Canadian Chronic Disease Surveillance System (CCDSS).
	>
	>DIMENSIONS	[SEX][FROM_25_TO_90_BY_5]
	>
	>SOURCE	Calibration to CCDSS diabetes prevalence rates.
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>

	>
	>*//* NOTE (DportAlterationSpecsAppliesToDiabetesPrevalenceFixSR, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER	Use altered DPoRT linear predictor in correction for diabetes
	prevalence
	>LOCATION	Base(Diabetes).dat
	>LAST UPDATE
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	This parameter introduces an additional age*sex term in DPoRT's
	equation so that POHEM rates of diabetes better align with target  from
	Canadian Chronic Disease Surveillance System (CCDSS).
	>
	>DIMENSIONS	None
	>
	>SOURCE	Calibration to CCDSS diabetes prevalence rates.
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS	A method is used to ensure that the distribution of initial diabetes
	status of people entering the simulation in 2001 agrees with that of parameter
	"Diabetes prevalence by age, sex and province for year 2001".  The actual
	method used depend on the parameter "Correspondence between initial diabetes
	alteration and diabetes risk" (called w thereafter).  If w ? 0  and |w| ? 1,
	the method will take into consideration a DPoRT-like risk to decide who needs
	their initial diabetes status to be altered (see "Correspondence between
	initial diabetes alteration and diabetes risk" for more details).
	>
	>The parameter currently discussed ("Use altered DPoRT linear predictor in
	correction for diabetes prevalence") tells POHEM if the aforementioned
	DPoRT-like risk  should be altered by the parameter "Alteration of DPoRT linear
	predictor by sex and age".
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS	Set to false.
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>
	>
	>*//* NOTE (DiabetesPrevalenceTarget2001, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER	Diabetes prevalence by age, sex and province for year 2001
	>LOCATION	Base(DiabetesPrevalenceStart).dat
	>LAST UPDATE	March 2019
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	Synthetic diabetes prevalence rate by province, sex and age group
	according to Canadian Chronic Disease Surveillance System (CCDSS).
	>
	>DIMENSIONS
	>[SEX][FROM_20_TO_80_BY_5][PROVINCE]
	>
	>
	>SOURCE	Two sets of broad prevalence rates from CCDSS were used to create
	detailed synthetic rates through raking.  The first broad set was prevalence by
	sex and fine age groups.  The second broad set was prevalence by province, sex
	and broad age groups.  The detailed synthetic rates are by province, sex and
	detailed age groups.
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS	POHEM comes with a file based on CCHS that provides the joint
	distribution of people's attributes (e.g. BMI, diabetes, sex, age, etc…).  This
	file (see parameter "Distribution of population at beginning of simulation") is
	used to initialise people's initial attributes as they enter POHEM's
	simulation. For diabetes, CCHS provides information on the prevalence of
	self-reported diabetes.  We may like POHEM to reflect a different diabetes
	concept at initialisation.
	>
	>The parameter "Diabetes prevalence by age, sex and province for year 2001"
	currently discussed is used to provide an alternative distribution of diabetes
	(and hence concept).  At the beginning of their POHEM life, people's diabetes
	status will tentatively be initialised based on "Distribution of population at
	beginning of simulation", but a few actors will see this tentative diabetes
	status assignment altered in order to achieve the prevalence found in the
	parameter "Diabetes prevalence by age, sex and province for year 2001".
	Thereafter, the diabetes status will be subject to incidence rates (see
	parameter "Use DPoRT algorithm for diabetes incidence").
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>

	>*//* NOTE (WeightForUnderlyingDPoRTishUniform, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER
	>Correspondence between initial diabetes alteration and diabetes risk
	>
	>LOCATION	Base(DiabetesPrevalenceStart).dat
	>LAST UPDATE	January 2019
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	The parameter allows users to select the methods used to ensure
	that POHEM's initial diabetes distribution agrees with some target.
	>
	>DIMENSIONS
	>None
	>
	>SOURCE
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS	The parameter "Initial AMI alteration : Correspondence with AMI risk"
	(called w thereafter) is used to tell which method to use to insure that
	initial diabetes prevalence lines up with some target.
	>* If |w| > 1 then the initial AMI alteration is turned off.
	>* If |w| ? 1 then a method is used to ensure that the distribution of initial
	diabetes status of people entering the simulation in 2001 agrees with that of
	parameter "Diabetes prevalence by age, sex and province for year 2001". The
	method used ensures that targets are met with minimal alteration to people's
	diabetes status.  The scope of this adjustment is restricted by parameters "In
	scope populations for initial diabetes alteration" and "In scope years for
	initial diabetes alteration"
	>
	>The results will depend on w and has an impact on who will see their initial
	diabetes status altered.  Here are some key values
	>* w=1: If in a given province, sex, age group category more diabetics are
	required then those who are not diabetic but are at high risk of diabetes
	according to a risk equation similar to DPoRT will be see their initial
	diabetes status change from NO to YES.  If, au contraire, fewer diabetics are
	required then those who are diabetic but are at low risk of diabetes according
	to a risk equation similar to DPoRT will be see their initial diabetes status
	change from YES to NO.
	>* w=-1: This does things backwards relative to w=1.  In a nutshell, change
	initial diabetes status from NO to YES if (DPoRT-like) risk of diabetes is low
	and from YES to NO if risk of diabetes is high.  This is a somewhat silly
	option.
	>* w=0: The (DPoRT-like) risk of diabetes is not used to decide who will get an
	altered initial diabetes status.
	>
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS	w=1
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>
	>

	>
	>*//* NOTE (DiabetesInitialAlterationPopulationScope, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER
	>In scope populations for initial diabetes alteration
	>
	>LOCATION	Base(DiabetesPrevalenceStart).dat
	>LAST UPDATE	March 2019
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	Populations over which the alignment of POHEM's initial diabetes
	status with some target is done.
	>
	>DIMENSIONS
	>[cPopulationSource]
	>
	>
	>SOURCE
	>
	>DEFINITION	In POHEM, there are 3 points of entry: (i) 2001, (ii) new births
	after 2001, and (iii) new immigrants after 2001.
	>
	>METHODS	Parameters "In scope populations for initial diabetes alteration" and
	"In scope years for initial diabetes alteration" tell POHEM who is subject to
	alteration of their diabetes status read in from the StartPop file (parameter
	"Distribution of population at beginning of simulation").
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>

	>
	>*//* NOTE (DiabetesInitialAlterationYearScope, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER
	>In scope years for initial diabetes alteration
	>
	>LOCATION	Base(DiabetesPrevalenceStart).dat
	>LAST UPDATE	March 2019
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	Years over which the alignment of POHEM's initial diabetes status
	with some target is done.
	>
	>DIMENSIONS
	>[cLOWER_UPPER_BOUNDS]
	>
	>
	>SOURCE
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS	Parameters "In scope populations for initial diabetes alteration" and
	"In scope years for initial diabetes alteration" tell POHEM who is subject to
	alteration of their diabetes status read in from the StartPop file (parameter
	"Distribution of population at beginning of simulation").
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>
	>*/

	/* NOTE (BuiltInBmiAlterationSpecs, EN )
