	/* NOTE (DppAdherencePropensityCorrelation, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER
	>Correlation in propensity to adhere to DPP 3 aspects
	>LOCATION	Base(DPP_Intervention).dat
	>LAST UPDATE	June 2016
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	This parameter control how propensity to adhere to the 3 aspects
	of DPP (BMI, physical activity and metformin) are correlated.
	>
	>DIMENSIONS	[SEX]
	>
	>SOURCE
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS	Suppose that parameters "Adherence for BMI" and "Adherence for PA" are
	populated in such a way that, 5 years into the program, 40% are supposed to
	still adhere to the BMI change/reduction and 40% are still supposed to adhere
	to physical activity (PA) change/increase.
	>
	>If the parameter is set to 0 (no correlation), adherence to PA is independent
	from adherence to BMI. This means that there will be about
	>* 0.4*0.4 = 16% of people still adhering to both BMI and PA
	>* 0.4*0.6 = 24% of people still adhering to BMI but not PA
	>* 0.6*0.4 = 24% of people still adhering to PA but not BMI
	>* 0.6*0.6 = 36% of people who no longer adhere to BMI nor PA.
	>
	>If the parameter is set to 1, adherence to PA is (perfectly) correlated to
	adherence to BMI. This means that there will be about
	>* 40% of people still adhering to both BMI and PA
	>* 60% of people who no longer adhere to BMI nor PA
	>
	>If the parameter is between 0 and 1, so correlation in adherence will be
	generated, but not as strong as the case above (corr=1).
	>
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS	Value in base scenario: 0.
	>
	>DECISIONS	The value chosen is more in line with previous versions of POHEM.
	>
	>REFERENCES	None.
	>RELATED PARAMETERS or VARIABLES
	>COMMENTS	Note that this correlation parameter is expected to be non-negative.
	If a negative value is provided (e.g. -0.75), the effect will be similar (but
	not identical) to providing its absolute value (0.75).  If the absolute value
	of the parameter is greater than 1, an error message will be issued.
	>*/


	/* NOTE (CvdportAmiBaseline, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER	CVDPoRT-AMI 5 year baseline cumulative hazard
	>LOCATION
	>LAST UPDATE	August 2019
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	This parameter provides information about the baseline hazard of
	AMI when time since interview is used as time in the hazard modeling
	>
	>DIMENSIONS	Sex
	>
	>SOURCE	In house/custom estimation of a variation of the model published in
	Manuel et al.
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS	See METHODS for “CVDPoRT-AMI BMI cubic spline companion”.
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>REFERENCES	Douglas G. Manuel MD, Meltem Tuna PhD, Carol Bennett MSc, Deirdre
	Hennessy PhD, Laura Rosella PhD, Claudia Sanmartin PhD, Jack V. Tu MD, Richard
	Perez MSc, Stacey Fisher MSc, Monica Taljaard PhD. Development and validation
	of a cardiovascular disease risk-prediction model using population health
	surveys: the Cardiovascular Disease Population Risk Tool (CVDPoRT). CMAJ 2018
	July 23;190:E871-82. doi: 10.1503/cmaj.170914.
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>
	>
	>*/ /* NOTE (CvdportAmiLogBaseCumulativeHazard, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER	CVDPoRT-AMI log baseline cumulative hazard
	>LOCATION
	>LAST UPDATE	August 2019
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	This parameter provides information about the baseline hazard of
	AMI when time since birth is used as time in the hazard modeling
	>
	>DIMENSIONS	Sex, Main non-spline terms
	>
	>SOURCE	In house/custom estimation of a variation of the model published in
	Manuel et al.
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS	See METHODS for “CVDPoRT-AMI BMI cubic spline companion”.
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>REFERENCES	Douglas G. Manuel MD, Meltem Tuna PhD, Carol Bennett MSc, Deirdre
	Hennessy PhD, Laura Rosella PhD, Claudia Sanmartin PhD, Jack V. Tu MD, Richard
	Perez MSc, Stacey Fisher MSc, Monica Taljaard PhD. Development and validation
	of a cardiovascular disease risk-prediction model using population health
	surveys: the Cardiovascular Disease Population Risk Tool (CVDPoRT). CMAJ 2018
	July 23;190:E871-82. doi: 10.1503/cmaj.170914.
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>
	>
	>*/ /* NOTE (CvdportAmiMainTermsMeans, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER	CVDPoRT-AMI main non-spline terms means
	>LOCATION
	>LAST UPDATE	August 2019
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	This parameter provides information about the mean of main
	non-spline variables involved in the hazard of AMI
	>
	>DIMENSIONS	Sex, Main non-spline terms
	>
	>SOURCE	In house/custom estimation of a variation of the model published in
	Manuel et al.
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
