	>
	>*/ /* NOTE (CvdportAmiBmiSplineLocationCoef, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER
	>  	CVDPoRT-AMI BMI cubic spline coefficients
	>LOCATION
	>LAST UPDATED
	>
	>DESCRIPTION
	>	This parameter provides information about a person’s BMI contribution to the
	hazard of AMI
	>
	>DIMENSIONS
	>	Sex, Cubic spline knots location and coefficient, List of knots
	>
	>SOURCE
	>	In house/custom estimation of a variation of the model published in Manuel et
	al.
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>
	>METHODS
	>	See METHODS for “CVDPoRT-AMI BMI cubic spline companion”.
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>	Douglas G. Manuel MD, Meltem Tuna PhD, Carol Bennett MSc, Deirdre Hennessy
	PhD, Laura Rosella PhD, Claudia Sanmartin PhD, Jack V. Tu MD, Richard Perez
	MSc, Stacey Fisher MSc, Monica Taljaard PhD. Development and validation of a
	cardiovascular disease risk-prediction model using population health surveys:
	the Cardiovascular Disease Population Risk Tool (CVDPoRT). CMAJ 2018 July
	23;190:E871-82. doi: 10.1503/cmaj.170914
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>
	>
	>
	>
	>
	>*/ /* NOTE (CvdportAmiBmiSplineCompanion, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER	CVDPoRT-AMI BMI cubic spline companion
	>LOCATION
	>LAST UPDATE	August 2019
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	This parameter provides information about a person’s BMI
	contribution to the hazard of AMI
	>
	>DIMENSIONS	Sex, Cubic spline companion
	>
	>SOURCE	In house/custom estimation of a variation of the model published in
	Manuel et al.
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS	The hazard of AMI is specified as
	>  log(hazard) = base  + age*sex calibration terms
	>      + Non-Spline terms + Non-Spline interaction terms
	>       + LT(age) + LT(BMI) + LT(Pack-years) + LT(Alcohol) + LT(METs)
	>       + CS(age) + CS(BMI) + CS(Pack-years) + CS(Alcohol) + CS(METs)
	>The base is log(K/5) where K is the content of “CVDPoRT-AMI 5 year baseline
	cumulative hazard” provided that K is not negative.  If K is negative, base is
	log( H0 (age+1) - H0 (age)   ) where H0 (age)  = exp(log(H0 (age) ))  with
	log(H0 (age) ) and log(H0 (age+1) ) are provided by the piecewise linear
	function depicted in parameter “CVDPoRT-AMI log baseline cumulative hazard”.
	>The “age*sex calibration terms” comes from parameter “Alteration of
	CVDPoRT-AMI log hazard by sex and age”.
	>“Non-Spline terms” has the form sum coefficient * (covariate - mean_covariate)
	with coefficient and mean_covariate provided in parameters “CVDPoRT-AMI main
	non-spline terms coefficients” and “CVDPoRT-AMI main non-spline terms means”
	respectively.
	>“Non-Spline interaction terms” has the form
								 sum coefficient * (covariate - mean_covariate) *
	(age – mean_age)  with coefficients and mean_covariate available in parameter
	“CVDPoRT-AMI interaction terms” and “CVDPoRT-AMI main non-spline terms means”
	respectively, whereas mean_age is available in “CVDPoRT-AMI age cubic splines
	companion”.
	>The CS(.) terms are the cubic splines which come with associated linear term
	LT(.). In those terms, age is integer age, alcohol is number of drinks in the
	past week and METs is Metabolic equivalent of task from leisure time physical
	activity, BMI is body mass index and Pack-years is pack-year smoked in lifetime
	(1 pack-year is 365 packs of cigarettes). Below, any of those five variables is
	denoted by x and <…>.  LT and CS described below are sex specific but this is
	not reflected in notation to ease reading.
	>LT(x) = coefficient * (coerced(x) – offset_linear) where coerced(x) is x
	restricted to be no less than A and no more than B, i.e.
	coerce(x)=max(min(x,B),A).  The parameter “CVDPoRT-AMI <…> cubic splines
	companion” contains A, B, offset_linear and the coefficient.
	>CS(x) = sum coefficient(k) * CS((coerced(x) – offset_cubic)/power_range,k)
	with CS(z,k) being the kth restricted cubic spline as described in
	5621-2016.pdf (see reference),  coefficient(k) being the corresponding
	coefficient, and power_range being (last knot – first knot)^power with power
	provided in paramter “CVDPoRT-AMI <…> cubic splines companion”. The sum is
	taken over the first K-2 knots where K is the total number of knots provided.
	The parameter “CVDPoRT-AMI <…> cubic splines coefficient” provides the location
	of the K knots (locations are assumed to be in ascending order) and the
	coefficients, whereas coerced(x) is the same as what was explained in the
	description of LT(x) above. The parameter “CVDPoRT-AMI <…> cubic splines
	companion” contains offset_cubic.
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>REFERENCES	Restricted Cubic Spline Regression: A Brief Introduction
	https://support.sas.com/resources/papers/proceedings16/5621-2016.pdf
	>Douglas G. Manuel MD, Meltem Tuna PhD, Carol Bennett MSc, Deirdre Hennessy
	PhD, Laura Rosella PhD, Claudia Sanmartin PhD, Jack V. Tu MD, Richard Perez
	MSc, Stacey Fisher MSc, Monica Taljaard PhD. Development and validation of a
	cardiovascular disease risk-prediction model using population health surveys:
	the Cardiovascular Disease Population Risk Tool (CVDPoRT). CMAJ 2018 July
	23;190:E871-82. doi: 10.1503/cmaj.170914.
	>
	>COMMENTS	Note that if one uses 0 or 2/3 as the “Power of knot range for
	scaling” in the parameter “CVDPoRT-AMI <…> cubic splines companion”, this will
	reproduce the results of the function rcspline.eval in the R package Hmisc with
	parameter norm set 0 and 2 respectively. The LT terms would typically arise
	from using inclx=TRUE in the rcspline.eval.
	>
	>*//* NOTE (CVDportAmiAlterationSpecs, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER	Alteration of CVDPoRT-AMI log hazard by sex and age
	>LOCATION
	>LAST UPDATE
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	This parameter introduces an additional age*sex term in
	CVDPoRT-AMI’s equation so that POHEM rates of AMI better align with target.
	>
	>DIMENSIONS	[SEX][FROM_25_TO_90_BY_5]
	>
	>SOURCE	Calibration to AMI prevalence rates (probably not Canadian Chronic
	Disease Surveillance System (CCDSS)).
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>
	>
	>*//* NOTE (CVDportAmiAlterationSpecsAppliesToPrevalenceFixSR, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER	Use altered CVDPoRT-AMI log hazard in correction for initial AMI
	prevalence
	>LOCATION
	>LAST UPDATE
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	This parameter indicates whether the additional age*sex term in
	CVDPoRT-AMI’s equation (see parameter “Alteration of CVDPoRT-AMI log hazard by
	sex and age”) should be used to for the purpose of evaluating risk in the
	correction of initial self-reported AMI.
	>
	>DIMENSIONS	None
	>
	>SOURCE
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS	A method is used to ensure that the distribution of initial AMI status
	of people entering the simulation in 2001 agrees with that of parameter
	“Initial AMI alteration : Prevalence by age, sex and province for year 2001”.
	The actual method used depend on the parameter “Initial AMI alteration :
	Correspondence with AMI risk” (called w thereafter).  If w != 0  and |w| <= 1,
	the method will take into consideration a CVDPoRT-like risk to decide who needs
	their initial AMI status to be altered.
	>
	>The parameter currently discussed (“Use altered CVDPoRT-AMI log hazard in
	correction for initial AMI prevalence”) tells POHEM if the aforementioned
	CVDPoRT-like risk should be altered by the parameter “Alteration of CVDPoRT-AMI
	log hazard by sex and age”.
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS	Set to false.
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>
	>*//* NOTE (AmiCvdportPrevalenceTarget2001, EN)
	>
	>PARAMETER	Initial AMI alteration : Prevalence by age, sex and province for
	year 2001
	>LOCATION
	>LAST UPDATE	September 2019
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	Synthetic AMI prevalence rate by province, sex and age group
	according to Canadian Chronic Disease Surveillance System (CCDSS).
	>
	>DIMENSIONS
	>[SEX][FROM_20_TO_80_BY_5][PROVINCE]
	>
	>
	>SOURCE	Two sets of broad prevalence rates from CCDSS were used to create
	detailed synthetic rates through raking.  The first broad set was prevalence by
	sex and fine age groups.  The second broad set was prevalence by province, sex
	and broad age groups.  The detailed synthetic rates are by province, sex and
	detailed age groups.
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS	POHEM comes with a file based on CCHS that provides the joint
	distribution of people’s attributes (e.g. BMI, diabetes, sex, age, etc…).  This
	file (see parameter “Distribution of population at beginning of simulation”) is
	used to initialise people’s initial attributes as they enter POHEM’s
	simulation. For AMI, CCHS provides information on the prevalence of
	self-reported AMI.  We may like POHEM to reflect a different AMI concept at
	initialisation.
	>
	>The parameter “Initial AMI alteration : Prevalence by age, sex and province
	for year 2001” currently discussed is used to provide an alternative
	distribution of AMI (and hence concept).  At the beginning of their POHEM life,
	people’s AMI status will tentatively be initialised based on “Distribution of
	population at beginning of simulation”, but a few actors will see this
	tentative AMI status assignment altered in order to achieve the prevalence
	found in the parameter “Initial AMI alteration : Prevalence by age, sex and
	province for year 2001”.  Thereafter, the AMI status will be subject to
	incidence rates (see parameter “CVDPoRT-AMI BMI cubic spline companion”).
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>
	>
	>*//* NOTE (WeightForUnderlyingAmiCvdportishUniform, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER
	>Initial AMI alteration : Correspondence with AMI risk
	>
	>LOCATION
	>LAST UPDATE	September 2019
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	The parameter allows users to select the methods used to ensure
	that POHEM’s initial AMI distribution agrees with some target.
	>
	>DIMENSIONS
	>None
	>
	>SOURCE
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS	The parameter “Initial AMI alteration : Correspondence with AMI risk”
	(called w thereafter) is used to tell which method to use to insure that
	initial AMI prevalence lines up with some target.
	>* 	If |w| > 1 then the initial AMI alteration is turned off.
	>* 	If |w| <= 1 then a method is used to ensure that the distribution of initial
	AMI status of people entering the simulation in 2001 agrees with that of
	parameter “Initial AMI alteration : prevalence by age, sex and province for
	year 2001”. The method used ensures that targets are met with minimal
	alteration to people’s AMI status.  The scope of this adjustment is restricted
	by parameters “Initial AMI alteration : In scope populations” and “Initial AMI
	alteration : In scope years”
	>
	>The results will depend on w and has an impact on who will see their initial
	AMI status altered.  Here are some key values
	>* 	w=1: If in a given province, sex, age group category more people with AMI
	are required then those who did not have an AMI but are at high risk of AMI
	according to a risk equation similar to CVDPoRT will be see their initial AMI
	status change from NO to YES.  If, au contraire, fewer people with AMI are
	required then those who had an AMI but are at low risk of AMI according to a
	risk equation similar to CVDPoRT will be see their initial AMI status change
	from YES to NO.
	>* 	w=-1: This does things backwards relative to w=1.  In a nutshell, change
	initial AMI status from NO to YES if (CVDPoRT-like) risk of AMI is low and from
	YES to NO if risk of AMI is high.  This is a somewhat silly option.
	>* 	w=0: The (CVDPoRT-like) risk of AMI is not used to decide who will get an
	altered initial AMI status.
	>
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS	w=1.  (Considered w=12, i.e. turn off, as we unsure if we have
	proper targets.)
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>
	>
	>
	>*//* NOTE (AmiCvdportInitialAlterationPopulationScope, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER
	>Initial AMI alteration: In scope populations
	>
	>LOCATION
	>LAST UPDATE	September 2019
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	Populations over which the alignment of POHEM’s initial AMI status
	with some target is done.
	>
	>DIMENSIONS
	>[cPopulationSource]
	>
	>
	>SOURCE
	>
	>DEFINITION	In POHEM, there are 3 points of entry: (i) 2001, (ii) new births
	after 2001, and (iii) new immigrants after 2001.
	>
	>METHODS	Parameters “Initial AMI alteration : In scope populations” and
	“Initial AMI alteration : In scope years” tell POHEM who is subject to
	alteration of their AMI status read in from the StartPop file (parameter
	“Distribution of population at beginning of simulation”).
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>
	>
