	/* NOTE (DementiaBmiSplineLocationCoef, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER	DEMPORT BMI cubic spline coefficients
	>LOCATION	Base(Dementia).dat
	>LAST UPDATE	June 2021
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	This parameter provides information about a person’s BMI
	contribution to the hazard of dementia
	>
	>DIMENSIONS	Sex, Cubic spline knots location and coefficient, List of knots
	>
	>SOURCE
	https://github.com/Big-Life-Lab/PoRT-cchsshared-dementia-v1-PBLwebsite-imp
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS	See METHODS for “DEMPORT BMI cubic spline companion”.
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>REFERENCES
	https://github.com/Big-Life-Lab/PoRT-cchsshared-dementia-v1-PBLwebsite-imp
	>
	>COMMENTS	A maximum of 20 knots are allowed in this parameter. A value of -9 is
	used for unneeded knot locations.
	>
	>
	>
	>
	>
	>
	>*/ /* NOTE (DementiaBmiSplineCompanion, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER	DEMPORT BMI cubic spline companion
	>LOCATION	Base(Dementia).dat
	>LAST UPDATE	June 2021
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	This parameter provides information about a person’s BMI
	contribution to the hazard of dementia
	>
	>DIMENSIONS	Sex, Cubic spline companion
	>
	>SOURCE
	https://github.com/Big-Life-Lab/PoRT-cchsshared-dementia-v1-PBLwebsite-imp
	>
	>DEFINITION	The spline companions included in the parameter are: (i) lower
	bound for entity used to compute spline; (ii) upper bound for entity used to
	compute spline; (iii) power of knot range used for scaling; (iv) center linear
	term; (v) coefficient for linear term; (vi) center cubic spline term.
	>
	>METHODS	The hazard of dementia is specified as
	>  log(hazard) = base  + age*sex calibration terms
	>      + Non-Spline terms + Non-Spline interaction terms
	>       + LT(age) + LT(BMI) + LT(Pack-years) + LT(Alcohol) + LT(METs)
	>       + CS(age) + CS(BMI) + CS(Pack-years) + CS(Alcohol) + CS(METs)
	>The base is log(K/5) where K is the content of “DEMPORT 5 year baseline
	cumulative hazard”, provided that K is not negative. If K is negative, the base
	is log(H0(age+1) - H0(age)) where H0(age) = exp(log(H0 (age))) with
	log(H0(age)) and log(H0 (age+1) ) are provided by the piecewise linear function
	depicted in parameter “DEMPORT log baseline cumulative hazard”.
	>
	>The “age*sex calibration terms” comes from parameter “Alteration of DEMPORT
	log hazard by sex and age”.
	>“Non-Spline terms” has the form sum coefficient * (covariate - mean_covariate)
	with coefficient and mean_covariate provided in parameters “DEMPORT main
	non-spline terms coefficients” and “DEMPORT main non-spline terms means”,
	respectively.
	>“Non-Spline interaction terms” has the form
								 sum coefficient * (covariate - mean_covariate) *
	(age – mean_age)  with coefficients and mean_covariate available in parameter
	“DEMPORT interaction terms” and “DEMPORT main non-spline terms means”,
	respectively, whereas mean_age is available in “DEMPORT age cubic splines
	companion”.
	>The CS(.) terms are the cubic splines, which come with associated linear term,
	LT(.). In those terms, age is integer age, alcohol is number of drinks in the
	past week, METs is Metabolic equivalent of task from leisure time physical
	activity, BMI is body mass index, and Pack-years is pack-year smoked in
	lifetime (1 pack-year is 365 packs of cigarettes). Below, any of those five
	variables is denoted by x and <…>.  LT and CS described below are sex-specific,
	but this is not reflected in notation to ease reading.
	>LT(x) = coefficient * (coerced(x) – offset_linear) where coerced(x) is x
	restricted to be no less than A and no more than B, i.e.,
	coerce(x)=max(min(x,B),A).  The parameter “DEMPORT <…> cubic splines companion”
	contains A, B, offset_linear and the coefficient.
	>CS(x) = sum coefficient(k) * CS((coerced(x) – offset_cubic)/power_range,k)
	with CS(z,k) being the kth restricted cubic spline as described in
	5621-2016.pdf (see reference),  coefficient(k) being the corresponding
	coefficient, and power_range being (last knot – first knot)^power with power
	provided in paramter “DEMPORT <…> cubic splines companion”. The sum is taken
	over the first K-2 knots where K is the total number of knots provided. The
	parameter “DEMPORT <…> cubic splines coefficient” provides the location of the
	K knots (locations are assumed to be in ascending order) and the coefficients,
	whereas coerced(x) is the same as what was explained in the description of
	LT(x) above. The parameter “DEMPORT <…> cubic splines companion” contains
	offset_cubic.
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>REFERENCES	Restricted Cubic Spline Regression: A Brief Introduction
	https://support.sas.com/resources/papers/proceedings16/5621-2016.pdf
	>https://github.com/Big-Life-Lab/PoRT-cchsshared-dementia-v1-PBLwebsite-imp
	>
	>COMMENTS	Note that if one uses 0 or 2/3 as the “Power of knot range for
	scaling” in the parameter “DEMPORT <…> cubic splines companion”, this will
	reproduce the results of the function rcspline.eval in the R package Hmisc with
	parameter norm set 0 and 2 respectively. The LT terms would typically arise
	from using inclx=TRUE in the rcspline.eval.
	>
	>
	>*//* NOTE (DementiaAlterationSpecs, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER	Alteration of DEMPORT log hazard by sex and age
	>LOCATION	Base(Dementia).dat
	>LAST UPDATE	June 2021
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	This parameter introduces an additional age*sex term in DemPoRT’s
	equation so that the POHEM incidence and/or prevalence rates of dementia better
	align with target.
	>
	>DIMENSIONS	[SEX][FROM_25_TO_90_BY_5]
	>
	>SOURCE	Calibration to dementia incidence rates as obtained from the Canadian
	Chronic Disease Surveillance System (CCDSS). Custom tabulations of CCDSS
	dementia incidence and prevalence data provided by smaller age groups at the
	national level.
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>
	>
	>*//* NOTE (DementiaAlterationSpecsAppliesToPrevalenceFixSR, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER	Use altered DEMPORT log hazard in correction for initial dementia
	prevalence
	>LOCATION
	>LAST UPDATE	June 2021
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	This parameter indicates whether the additional age*sex term in
	DemPoRT’s equation (see parameter “Alteration of DEMPORT log hazard by sex and
	age”) should be used to for the purpose of evaluating risk in the correction of
	initial self-reported dementia.
	>
	>DIMENSIONS	None
	>
	>SOURCE
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS	A method is used to ensure that the distribution of initial dementia
	status of people entering the simulation in 2001 agrees with that of parameter
	“Initial dementia alteration : Prevalence by age, sex and province for year
	2001”.  The actual method used depends on the parameter “Initial dementia
	alteration : Correspondence with dementia risk” (called w thereafter).  If w !=
	0  and |w| <= 1,  the method will take into consideration a DemPoRT-like risk to
	decide who needs their initial dementia status to be altered.
	>
	>The parameter currently discussed (“Use altered DEMPORT log hazard in
	correction for initial dementia prevalence”) tells POHEM if the aforementioned
	DemPoRT-like risk should be altered by the parameter “Alteration of DEMPORT log
	hazard by sex and age”.
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS	Set to false in base scenario.
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>
	>*//* NOTE (DementiaPrevalenceTarget2001, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER	Initial dementia alteration: Prevalence by age, sex and province for
	year 2001
	>LOCATION
	>LAST UPDATE	March 2021
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	Synthetic dementia prevalence rate by province, sex and age group
	derived based on data available in the Canadian Chronic Disease Surveillance
	System (CCDSS).
	>
	>DIMENSIONS
	>[SEX][FROM_20_TO_80_BY_5][PROVINCE]
	>
	>
	>SOURCE	CCDSS provides dementia prevalence (and incidence) data starting in
	2002 (www.health-infobase.canada.ca/ccdss/data-tool), therefore it was
	necessary to extrapolate estimates backwards to derive a target dementia
	prevalence rate for the year 2001. A custom tabulation of CCDSS dementia data
	was also made available for this project: dementia prevalence data was provided
	at the national level for more detailed age groups (65-69, 70-74, 75-79, 80-84,
	85-89, 90+), separately for males and females.
	>
	>DEFINITION	Dementia case counts from CCDSS were compared to population counts
	obtained from CANSIM in order to derive rates (proportions).
	>
	>METHODS	POHEM comes with a file based on 2001 CCHS that provides the joint
	distribution of people’s attributes (e.g., BMI, diabetes, sex, age, etc.).
	This file (see parameter “Distribution of population at beginning of
	simulation”) is used to initialise people’s initial attributes as they enter
	POHEM’s simulation. Although self-reported dementia was not originally provided
	as a stand-alone variable on the Public Use Microdata File for 2001, special
	permission was obtained to release and use this variable. Even so, it may be
	necessary to further adjust the reported prevalence of dementia in POHEM to
	reflect a different dementia concept at initialisation.
	>
	>The parameter “Initial dementia alteration: Prevalence by age, sex and
	province for year 2001” currently discussed is used to provide an alternative
	distribution of dementia (and hence concept).  At the beginning of their POHEM
	life, people’s dementia status will tentatively be initialised based on
	“Distribution of population at beginning of simulation”, but a few actors will
	see this tentative dementia status assignment altered in order to achieve the
	prevalence found in the parameter “Initial dementia alteration: Prevalence by
	age, sex and province for year 2001”.  Thereafter, the dementia status will be
	subject to incidence rates (see parameter “DEMPORT BMI cubic spline
	companion”).
	>
	>To estimate the dementia prevalence target for 2001, CCDSS dementia prevalence
	rates were examined separately by sex, age group and province for years 2002
	through 2016, which is the most recent year available in CCDSS. In plotting
	these, it appeared as though the trend was linear in the early years of CCDSS.
	As such, a linear model was used to predict 2001 prevalence rates for each
	combination of sex, age group, and province using CCDSS observed data for years
	2002 through 2010, inclusive.
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS	CCDSS does not obtain or provide dementia incidence or prevalence
	data for Saskatchewan. In POHEM, age- and sex-specific dementia prevalence
	target rates from Manitoba were used as proxies for Saskatchewan.
	>
	>DECISIONS	The CCDSS only collects dementia incidence and prevalence
	information for Canadians age 65 years and older. DemPoRT evaluates the risk of
	dementia for individuals age 55 years and older, and the 2001 CCHS asked for
	self-reported dementia for respondents age 18 years and older. Therefore, there
	are some age scope discrepancies between the various sources of dementia data.
	In setting the dementia prevalence in POHEM in 2001, it was decided (based on
	low dementia incidence and prevalence rates for individuals younger than age 65
	in CCDSS) that any self-reported dementia prevalence from 2001 CCHS for
	individuals under age 65 would be set to zero based on the parameter “Initial
	dementia alteration: Prevalence by age, sex and province for year 2001”.
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>
	>
	>
	>*//* NOTE (DementiaInitialAlterationPopulationScope, EN )
	>
	>PARAMETER
	>Initial dementia alteration: In scope populations
	>
	>LOCATION
	>LAST UPDATE	June 2021
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	Populations over which the alignment of POHEM’s initial dementia
	status with some target is done.
	>
	>DIMENSIONS
	>[cPopulationSource]
	>
	>
	>SOURCE
	>
	>DEFINITION	In POHEM, there are 3 points of entry: (i) 2001, (ii) new births
	after 2001, and (iii) new immigrants after 2001.
	>
	>METHODS	Parameters “Initial dementia alteration: In scope populations” and
	“Initial dementia alteration: In scope years” tell POHEM who is subject to
	alteration of their dementia status read in from the StartPop file (parameter
	“Distribution of population at beginning of simulation”).
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>
	>
